<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-dopa and L-dopachrome biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi_pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Tyrosinases, widely scattered among animals, plants and fungi, are involved in many biologically essential functions such as pigmentation, sclerotization, primary immune response and host defence. The tyrosinases use molecular oxygen to catalyze two different enzymatic reactions - the &lt;i&gt;ortho&lt;/i&gt;-hydroxylation of monophenols to &lt;i&gt;ortho&lt;/i&gt;-diphenols (monophenolase, cresolase acticity) and the oxidation of &lt;i&gt;ortho&lt;/i&gt;-diphenols to &lt;i&gt;ortho&lt;/i&gt;-quinones (diphenolase, catecholase activity). They belong to the family of class 3 copper proteins, which contain two copper atoms at the active site, each stabilized by three |FRAME: HIS| residues |CITS: [10820445]|. The mammalian |FRAME: HS01248-MONOMER| is a melanocyte-specific enzyme critical for the synthesis of |FRAME: MELANIN|, which takes place in a post-Golgi compartment termed the melanosome. It converts |FRAME: TYR| to |FRAME: L-DIHYDROXY-PHENYLALANINE| and |FRAME: L-DIHYDROXY-PHENYLALANINE| to |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE|. Once formed, |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE| can spontaneously rearrange to form the cyclic |FRAME: CPD-8652|, and the later can spontaneously react with a second molecule of |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE|, forming |FRAME: L-DIHYDROXY-PHENYLALANINE| and |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME|. |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME| is the building block for melanin. This molecule is subject to both enzymatic and non-enzymatic oxidation and polymerization steps that lead to formation of the many different types of melanins that are found in living organisms. Melanin production is also present in bacteria. For example, many Rhizobium species produce melanin pigments when grown on complete media |CITS: [8366027]|. In |FRAME: TAX-382| the mepA gene, which encodes the tyrosinase, is present on the large (140 MDa) plasmid pRmeGR4b. Several roles for melanin in bacteria have been proposed, including detoxifying phenolc compounds in |FRAME: TAX-385| |CITS: [3474493]|, prevention of cellular dehydration through sequestration of compatible solutes in |FRAME: TAX-666| |CITS: [1444398]|, and induction of virulence factors by the generation of hydrogen peroxide, also in |FRAME: TAX-666| |CITS: [19103773]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="chawkins" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
